     Opts out of .section's shared max-width/centering (see the shared
     `main`/`.section` rule near the top of this file — same mechanism
     already used for .hero) so the track can bleed to the true viewport
     edges like compatto's own marquee does, instead of stopping at the
     1280px content cap every other section uses. `aria-hidden` on the
     whole section: it's decorative repetition of a line already spoken
     once, meaningfully, in the about section above — a screen reader
     looping through a dozen duplicate copies would add noise, not
     content. */
  .marquee-section {
    max-width: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--space-4xl) 0;
    border-top: none;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Two identical groups back-to-back, animated exactly -50% — once the
     first group has scrolled fully past, the second is sitting exactly
     where the first started, so the loop has no visible seam. `width:
     max-content` lets the track grow as wide as its content actually
     needs (double the real content width) rather than being capped to
     the viewport, which is what makes the -50% math line up. */
  .marquee-track {
    display: flex;
    width: max-content;
    /* 42s → 54.6s (owner's ask, 2026-09-13: 30% slower). */
    animation: marquee-scroll 54.6s linear infinite;
  }

  /* font-size lives here, not on .marquee-text — real bug found
     2026-09-13 (measured, not guessed: computed .marquee-mark width was
     8.8px, not the intended ~53px): .marquee-text and .marquee-mark are
     SIBLINGS, so an `em` unit on the mark was resolving against its OWN
     inherited font-size (the page default, ~16px) rather than the
     text's 96px — `em` always resolves against the element's own font-
     size, never a sibling's. Putting the size on their shared parent
     instead means both children inherit the same font-size context, so
     .marquee-mark's `em` sizing below actually tracks .marquee-text's
     visible size. Sized off the existing display-lg fluid scale, not a
     new token, but pushed taller still ("da bude visok", owner's
     explicit ask) — no locked type step goes this large, so this clamp
     is a deliberate new value scoped to this one decorative element,
     same category of exception as --gradient-brand: flagged here rather
     than invented quietly. */
  .marquee-group {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    gap: var(--space-2xl);
    padding-right: var(--space-2xl);
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 1.6rem + 5.5vw, 6rem);
  }

  .marquee-text {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    font-size: inherit;
    line-height: 1;
    font-weight: var(--text-display-lg-weight);
    letter-spacing: var(--text-display-lg-tracking);
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The logo mark (owner's ask, 2026-09-13: logo-mark.svg — the
     standalone text-free mark, not the Signature Shape), recolored via
     mask (same technique used site-wide for tinting this mark/shape
     family, e.g. .about-stats-mark, .about-stats::after) instead of an
     <img> — the source file has no fill/currentColor wiring of its own,
     so masking a flat color is how this codebase always tints it.
     `aspect-ratio` matches the file's own viewBox (174.79 × 168.95)
     exactly, same convention as `.about-stats::after`'s own ratio-match
     — `height` in `em` off the shared .marquee-group font-size above
     (see its comment) so it scales with the fluid text automatically,
     width follows from the ratio instead of being guessed separately.
     0.58em → 0.72em (owner's ask, 2026-09-14: size the mark to match
     the cap-height of the adjacent text's first letter, not just "a
